WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Speaker 1: Let's go to Mike in northeastern&nbsp;
Connecticut. Mike, you're on the air.&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Speaker 2: David, thank you for taking my call. I&nbsp;
had a question about ranked choice voting, OK, why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.600
Massachusetts didn't pass that in the last vote.
Speaker 1: Did that not pass in Massachusetts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.520
I don't remember there are&nbsp;
so many ballot initiatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.440
I can't remember all of them. I don't know&nbsp;
why it didn't pass, if that's the case, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.600
I've spoken about ranked choice voting a&nbsp;
number of times. There's a number of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.440
configurations of it, single transferable vote&nbsp;
and many others. I think the most important thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.760
when it comes to ranked choice voting is voters&nbsp;
need to be confident that there is no possible way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:48.240
that someone who really doesn't have a plurality&nbsp;
of voters wanting them to be the winner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.040
ends up as the winner. And it's sort of like a&nbsp;
mathematical problem, and we've done a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.200
interviews on this. There's a number of systems&nbsp;
that ensure that and that accomplish that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.680
And it's generally speaking a system I'm in favor&nbsp;
of with the caveat that I would like to see ranked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:13.520
choice voting and public financing of elections&nbsp;
and campaign finance reform because not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.240
is it important to generate votes for&nbsp;
third parties and additional parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.760
it would also be great for the main two parties&nbsp;
not to have such an overwhelming advantage when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.840
it comes to the money. So I think you need&nbsp;
to do both together. I'd also love to see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.160
national popular vote, and all of these things&nbsp;
together feel like pipe dreams, but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.280
the ideal that that I've sort of envisioned.
Speaker 2: Now, do you feel the voters would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.640
competent enough to fill more a regular ballot?
Speaker 1: I don't know. I mean, we do have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.560
competence and education problem in the United&nbsp;
States. I'm pretty confident that if people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.560
just get a piece of paper and it says there&nbsp;
are five candidates, rank them one to five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.440
that most voters would be able to do that&nbsp;
correctly. I'm modestly confident in that.&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.480
Speaker 2: OK, great. And do you ever&nbsp;
touch on the right to repair that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.040
another Massachusetts ballot initiative?
Speaker 1: I'm generally speaking in favor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:19.920
the right to repair. I've heard the arguments that&nbsp;
right to repair would allow people to get vehicles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.200
fixed or devices fixed by people who aren't&nbsp;
really qualified. But I don't really buy that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.000
I think you can deal with the qualification in&nbsp;
the certification. So my view is it's basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.760
a way to monopolize of service and repair&nbsp;
dollars, and I'm in favor of right to repair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.480
Great. Thank you for taking my&nbsp;
call. My pleasure, Mike, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:03:41.040
northeastern Connecticut. A&nbsp;
lot of interesting stuff there.

